# The two IIO sensors the kernel exposes (§48).
#
#   LIS2HH12  accelerometer  i2c-6 0x1d  -> iio:device3 (+ /dev/iio:device3)
#   CM3323    RGB light      i2c-6 0x10  -> iio:device2
#
# The IIO HAL (hardware/intel/sensors-iio, loaded by the multihal) discovers
# them by listing /sys/bus/iio/devices and then works on the device's sysfs
# attributes. AOSP grants hal_sensors_default no access to sysfs, because
# the reference HAL talks to a sensor hub, not to the kernel: without these
# lines the HAL starts, finds nothing and SensorService keeps saying
# "No Sensors on the device" — the difference being that now the denials
# explain why.

# /sys/bus/iio/devices is generic sysfs: the HAL lists the directory and
# follows the links to /sys/devices/...
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs:dir r_dir_perms;
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs:lnk_file read;

# The attributes of the two devices, labelled in genfs_contexts. Writable,
# not read-only: the HAL sets sampling_frequency and the calibbias values,
# and in trigger mode it would also write buffer/length and
# buffer/enable.
type sysfs_iio, fs_type, sysfs_type;
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs_iio:dir r_dir_perms;
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs_iio:file rw_file_perms;

# The character node. The HAL opens it **also** for polled sensors: that is
# how it keeps the hardware powered while the sensor is in use (the HAL's
# README, section "FDs and CHANNELS"). The iio_device label is AOSP's
# (public/device.te), the access rule is not.
allow hal_sensors_default iio_device:chr_file rw_file_perms;
